WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.011
A fact asserts that something is true in a case.

2
00:00:03.311 --> 00:00:07.812
A conditional rule says what would have to be true if its sufficient condition held.

3
00:00:08.112 --> 00:00:09.925
Every sealed sample is logged.

4
00:00:10.225 --> 00:00:11.569
Sample M is sealed.

5
00:00:11.869 --> 00:00:14.323
The first sentence covers any sealed sample.

6
00:00:14.623 --> 00:00:16.671
The second tells us that M is one.

7
00:00:16.971 --> 00:00:18.208
So M is logged.

8
00:00:18.508 --> 00:00:25.100
If the given fact instead said M was not logged, the requirement would be missing and M could not be sealed.

9
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:30.520
But logged alone would not prove sealed, and not sealed alone would not prove not logged.

10
00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:35.535
The same rule can be useful or inconclusive depending on the fact supplied.

11
00:00:35.835 --> 00:00:40.912
Keeping all four situations distinct prevents guesses from becoming conclusions.

12
00:00:41.212 --> 00:00:43.217
Write the facts for this same subject.

13
00:00:43.517 --> 00:00:50.067
A hypothetical sufficient condition is not an observed fact; do not fill it in from the rule’s existence.

14
00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:53.183
Name what is given before naming what follows.

15
00:00:53.483 --> 00:01:00.928
When a fact proves nothing about the other condition, leave that condition open and keep the rule available for later information.
